-On apply, TA prints one `[applied]` or `[failed]` line per proposal. Proposals are replayed only when a person is present: from an interactive terminal, or when you pass `--replay-actions`. A non-interactive apply (a script, CI, the session auto-apply gate, the release pipeline) applies the files, prints how many external actions were not replayed, and leaves them for you to run with `ta draft apply <id> --replay-actions`. `--no-replay-actions`, or `TA_NO_REPLAY_ACTIONS=1`, turns replay off even in a terminal. `ta draft apply <id> --chain --dry-run` previews the whole chain and changes and replays nothing. A failed proposal never blocks the others or the file changes; fix the cause and re-run `ta draft apply`. Re-applying is safe: replayed proposals are recorded in `.ta/wayfinder-task-replay.log` and skipped next time, and created tasks always carry an `external_id` (derived from the goal and proposal ids when the agent did not supply one), so a task is never created twice. A project without `[plan] backend = "wayfinder"` simply skips the proposals with a log line. The Wayfinder service account needs only `member` role for these calls.
+On apply, TA prints one `[applied]` or `[failed]` line per proposal. Proposals are replayed only when a person is present: from an interactive terminal, or when you pass `--replay-actions`. A non-interactive apply (a script, CI, the session auto-apply gate, the release pipeline) applies the files, prints how many external actions were not replayed, and leaves them for you to run with `ta draft apply <id> --replay-actions`. `--no-replay-actions`, or `TA_NO_REPLAY_ACTIONS=1`, turns replay off even in a terminal. `ta draft apply <id> --chain --dry-run` previews the whole chain and changes and replays nothing. A failed proposal never blocks the others or the file changes; fix the cause and re-run `ta draft apply`. Re-applying is safe: replayed proposals are recorded in `.ta/wayfinder-task-replay.log` and skipped next time, and created tasks always carry an `external_id` (derived from the goal and proposal ids when the agent did not supply one), so a task is never created twice. A project without `[plan] backend = "wayfinder"` simply skips the proposals with a log line. The Wayfinder service account needs the `tasks:write` right for these calls (the goal and KPI proposals below add `kpis:manage` for org-level KPIs).
+
+##### Proposing goals, KPI metrics and datapoints
+
+A project-manager worker can also propose Wayfinder goals and KPIs. They follow the same rule: the proposal rides in the draft a person reviews, and is written to Wayfinder only when the draft is approved and applied. The chief-of-staff chat mode has none of these tools.
+
+| Tool | Proposes | Wayfinder call | Right needed |
+|------|----------|----------------|--------------|
+| `ta_propose_goal_create` | A goal (`name` required; `description`, `kind`, `parent_goal_id`, `target_date` optional) | `POST projects/:id/goals` | `tasks:write` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_metric_create` | A KPI metric on a goal (`goal_id`, `label`, `unit`, `target`, `cadence`, `kind` required) | `POST projects/:id/goals/:goal_id/kpi-metrics` | `tasks:write` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_datapoint_add` | A datapoint on a metric (`metric_id`, `value`; `source` optional) | `POST projects/:id/kpi-metrics/:metric_id/datapoints` | `tasks:write` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_create` | An org-level KPI (`label`, `description`; `weight`, `project_scoped`, `source_ref` optional) | `POST orgs/:id/kpis` | `kpis:manage` |
+
+Every proposal needs the `goal_run_id` of the run it belongs to. Numbers must be finite and within plus or minus 1e12, and `cadence`, `kind` and `aggregation` must be one of the listed values; a bad proposal is refused when it is made, with the allowed values in the message. The project and org always come from the project's `[plan.wayfinder]` config, never from the proposal.
+
+To build on something created earlier in the same draft, use `ref:<n>` in place of an id, where `n` is the proposal number the tool returned (the first proposal is `ref:0`). For example, propose a goal (`ref:0`), a metric on `ref:0` (`ref:1`), then a datapoint on `ref:1`. A reference must point at an earlier proposal of the right kind.
+
+What a reviewer sees in `ta draft view`: one entry per proposal with the name, target, unit and cadence (or the value and metric), any `ref:<n>` it depends on, and the line "Written to Wayfinder only when this draft is approved and applied."
+
+On apply the proposals are replayed in the order shown. Each create sends an `external_id` of `ta_draft:<draft-id>:<n>`, so repeating it never creates a duplicate. The ids Wayfinder returns are recorded in `.ta/wayfinder-task-replay.log`, so `ref:<n>` still resolves when you re-run `ta draft apply` after a partial failure. If a step fails, the steps that depend on it are not attempted and the output names the step to fix. Common failures:
+
+- 403: the service account lacks `tasks:write` (goals, metrics, datapoints) or `kpis:manage` (org KPIs). Grant it in Wayfinder, then re-apply.
+- 409 on a datapoint: Wayfinder computes `task_count` metrics from tasks and refuses datapoints for them. The message is shown as Wayfinder sent it; use an `external` metric for hand-entered numbers.
+- 409 on a create: the `external_id` is already used by a different resource.
